course: dictionary
lesson: 14346
title: Offending: the one causing trouble
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Offending has more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one. Offending can describe a thing that causes a problem or breaks a rule. «The offending sock was hiding under the sofa again.» Nobody is upset by the sock. It is simply the one causing the trouble, so offending here means causing a problem.
explain: Offending can also mean upsetting someone or making them feel hurt or insulted. «Jamal's rude joke was offending, and Mia walked out of the room.» The joke hurt Mia's feelings, so offending means upsetting. Check it: swap in "upsetting" and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Offending comes from the verb offend, which has the forms offend, offends, offended and offending. «The guest was offended by the rude remark.» Offended is how the person feels. Offending is what causes the feeling. Remember that difference and you will not mix them up.
fact: Offending means upsetting someone or making them feel hurt or insulted.
fact: The offending thing is the one that causes a problem or breaks a rule.
fact: Offending can also mean breaking the law, and a person who breaks the law is called an offender.
fact: Offend is the verb; offending is its -ing form.
fact: In manners lessons, we learn to avoid offending people with rude words.
exercise: «The offending sock was hiding under the sofa again.» What does offending mean here? | *The one causing the trouble | The one that is very soft | The one that is clean | The one that was lost forever
exercise: «Jamal's rude joke was offending, and Mia walked out of the room.» What does offending mean? | Quiet and calm | *Upsetting and hurtful | Funny and clever | Very long
exercise: «The guest was offended by the rude remark.» Find and copy one word that is a form of the verb offend. | rude | *offended | guest | remark
test: A person who breaks the law is called an ... | sailor | *offender | helper
test: «The offending tooth was loose, so Sam wiggled it all day.» What is the offending tooth? | The tooth that is brand new | *The tooth causing the trouble | The tooth that fell out last week | The tooth that is very white
exam: Which meaning fits best in «The offending joke made everyone go silent»? | Very long | Brand new | *Upsetting or hurtful | Sung loudly
